628	doc.push(H3("9.5 — The lesson this stage adds"));
629	doc.push(P("Three of the five tasks in this session ended with an audit finding something the author's own tests could not, and every session in this project has now had that happen. The pattern is not carelessness. It is that the person who writes the claim also writes the test that agrees with it, and no amount of care inside that loop breaks the loop. The standing rule — a fresh agent told to falsify — is what has been doing the work, and Stage 9's head-line finding is the clearest case yet, because it survived a green suite that had been built specifically to attack the thing it was hiding in."));
630	doc.push(P("The second lesson is a document one, and it accumulated rather than arrived: most of this session's corrections were to prose, not code. The registry was repeatedly described as \"signed\" when it is hash-chained; a customer document claimed a property the export did not have; the rate card priced a registry as unbuilt that is now partly built. None of those would fail a test."));
631	
632	// ---- Stage 10
633	doc.push(H2("Stage 10 — chat-6: authenticated disposition entry, and a guard the audit found inert (2026-11-11)"));
634	doc.push(PB("Chat-6 opened with the owner restating a standing rule as a permanent one: every chat capped at 125,000 tokens, and every build phase laid out as a priority ladder from low complexity through high, worked one item at a time. The ladder for this phase had six rungs, and the chosen track was the item chat-5's handoff named as the largest remaining code item: **session-bound disposition entry**, the blocking half of B01 §7 item 1."));
635	
636	doc.push(H3("10.1 — What the gap was, stated precisely"));
637	doc.push(PB("Chat-5 built the reviewer registry and HMAC attestations. A disposition could be *bound* to a registered reviewer and the binding *verified* by a recipient of an export. But the write route read `reviewer_name` and `cert_id` out of the JSON body, so the name on a decision was whatever the caller typed. The claim that a reviewer made a decision was verifiable but not **enforced at the point of entry** — and a registry nobody is required to present is a registry that documents intent rather than gating action."));
638	
639	doc.push(H3("10.2 — What was built"));
640	doc.push(PB("`backend/accounts.py` binds an account at enrollment to a certification record already in the reviewer registry, and refuses enrollment for a certificate the registry does not have or has revoked. The account stores a PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 verifier — 600,000 iterations, a 16-byte per-account salt — rather than a passphrase. The platform's own dependency set contains no KDF, and hashlib's is used rather than writing one, for the reason the attribution scheme also gave: a product that tells CISOs it does not hand-roll crypto does not get to make an exception for the thing protecting the login."));
641	doc.push(PB("A session is a 32-byte bearer token of which only the SHA-256 digest is stored, so a copy of the database yields nothing usable. `resolve_session` checks five things, and the fifth is the one that is expensive to omit: the token was issued here, the session is not revoked, it has not expired, the account is still live, and **the certificate behind it is still live**. A certificate revoked at 09:00 would otherwise keep authorising writes until the token's TTL ran out, and every one of them would be attributed to a certificate that no longer existed."));
642	doc.push(PB("The account register is append-only and hash-chained with the same trigger set as the reviewer registry, and its hash **covers salt, iteration count and verifier**. That last part is not decoration: a host owner who could swap a verifier would own the account and every session it ever issued, so a value the hash does not cover would be a value somebody can change. The same reasoning as the registry's decision to hash `registered_at`."));
643	doc.push(PB("The route takes identity from the session and **nowhere else**, and a body carrying `reviewer_name` or `cert_id` is **refused rather than ignored**. That choice is the one integrators will notice: an ignored field lets a caller believe they named the reviewer, and the resulting code names the wrong person for as long as nobody re-reads the response. Refusing it turns a silent wrong answer into a 400 with a reason. With **no account enrolled the path closes (503)** instead of falling back to the old free-text behaviour — the fallback would make the gate only as strong as somebody remembering to enrol an account. `tools/seed_demo_account.py` is the deliberate act that opens it."));
644	doc.push(PB("`store.save_disposition` grew a `guard` callable run inside its write transaction, because the ledger's write lock is the only instant at which \"still live\" and \"still unwritten\" are both true. The same race that made `attest()` re-read under its lock in chat-5."));
645	
646	doc.push(H3("10.3 — The guard that could not fail, found by the audit that was told to falsify"));
647	doc.push(PB("An independent agent was given the claim — *a disposition cannot be written unless the caller presents a live session token, and the identity on the row comes from the session* — and told to falsify it rather than review the code. It falsified the first half in one line. **The revocation guard did nothing.** `save_disposition` called `guard()` and discarded the return value, while the route installed `lambda: accounts.cert_is_live(...)` — which *returns a boolean*. So the guard ran, computed `False`, and the row was written anyway. A certificate revoked between resolve and INSERT produced HTTP 200, a row, and a reviewer name under a certificate that no longer existed."));
648	doc.push(callout(
649	  "Why 310 green tests said nothing",
650	  "The test simulated the race with a guard that **raised**. That is not the guard the route installs. A guard whose only tested failure mode is one the caller does not use is not a guard; it is a guard-shaped object that the suite and the route agree about and the attacker does not. This is the fourth session running where an audit found something the author's tests could not, and the third in which the failing thing was newly built rather than old.",
651	  { fill: "FBEAEA", labelColor: RED },
652	));
653	doc.push(PB("The fix aborts on a falsy return, and the route turns that abort into a 401 rather than a 500. The new test revokes the certificate **inside** `resolve_session` — the real race, through the real route, not a synthetic guard — and reverting the fix reproduces the exploit verbatim: HTTP 200, row written, certificate already revoked. That reproduction is the evidence; a test that only passes is not."));
654	
655	doc.push(H3("10.4 — The hostile-input sweep"));
656	doc.push(PB("Two more defects came from asking what a malformed body does rather than what a valid one does. `{\"note\": {…}}` raised `AttributeError` on `.strip()`, and `{\"disposition\": [\"x\"]}` raised `TypeError` because `list in dict` hashes the key — both surfaced as a 500, both now 400 with a reason. The second is the same class as a defect another module in this repository already had, which is the argument for a sweep over body shapes rather than a fix per reported bug."));
657	
658	doc.push(H3("10.5 — What held, and what the stage deliberately does not claim"));
659	doc.push(PB("No credential path produced a write: absent header, bare token, `Basic`, the token replaced by its own digest, expired, signed out, account revoked. A token for reviewer A could not record a decision as B, and nine alternative body field names changed nothing. Duplicate JSON keys could not smuggle identity. Exactly one call path reaches `store.save_disposition`; the ingest, export and replication modules only read the ledger. A host owner with database write access can hand-insert a session row or rewrite an expiry, and the module says so instead of implying otherwise."));
660	doc.push(PB("Four limits are stated rather than implied. It does not prove a **human** was at the keyboard — it proves a credential was presented, and custody and joiner/mover/leaver are a procedure, not a function. It does not protect the **token in transit**: the demo host is plain HTTP, and a bearer token there is a shared secret on the wire (B01 §7 item 10). It does not make **attestation** stronger: passing the reviewer's HMAC key as the login credential was rejected precisely because it would put the attestation secret on the server for the life of every session. And entry now requires a live **session**, not an **attestation** — an unattested row is still an honest state the manifest counts."));
661	
662	doc.push(H3("10.6 — Documents this stage corrected"));
663	doc.push(P("Most of the prose corrections were the same overstatement in four places: that entry is not gated. B01 §7 item 1, B01's AD-2 architecture row, its \"what this does not claim\" item 2, D09 §4.4, D00 Parts IV and VI, and F08's purpose note all said a reviewer can self-assert a certification ID. Each was rewritten in the same pass as the code change, per a rule this docket repeats: a gap moves to closed only when the code changes and every document that discloses it is amended in the same commit. F08 changed character rather than status — it is no longer a substitute for a missing technical control, it is now the record of *who the person behind the credential is*, which software still cannot establish."));
664	doc.push(P("Verification for the stage: suite 238 to 315. Six guard mutations and five credential mutations attempted; all killed, one only as a pair, which is recorded in the code because a source-level guard is what closes it. The audit that was run last found the defect that the tests did not, which is the order this project has learned to run them in."));
665	
666	// ---- Stage 11
667	doc.push(H2("Stage 11 — chat-7: authenticated ingest, a portable key directory, and four mutations that outlived the tests (2026-11-11)"));
668	doc.push(PB("Chat-7 took the two AD-2 halves chat-6 left, in the order the handoff set out, one item at a time. The first was the larger gap in the whole product: **`POST /api/ingest` had no authentication at all.** Anyone who could reach the port could append rows to the ingest log, and the docket had carried the sentence \"an unauthenticated ingest route is a data-poisoning route\" as a warning rather than a description of a fixed thing."));
669	
670	doc.push(H3("11.1 — A collector is not a reviewer, and the credential model follows from that"));
671	doc.push(PB("The shortcut was to reuse the reviewer account table chat-6 had just built. It is the wrong shape, and the reason is the domain rather than the code. A reviewer is a person: the session expires in hours and dies if the certificate behind it is revoked, because a reviewer who has left should stop recording decisions immediately. A collector is scheduled software on a wayside detector. It runs for weeks, nobody signs it in, and revoking an enrollment because one detector was decommissioned should not break a running collector mid-shift without an operator deciding to. One credential table for both would have made the two lifecycles share a single fate, and the auditor's question — is this the same credential — would have had no clean answer."));
672	doc.push(PB("So `backend/ingest_auth.py` gives collectors their own register. Enrollment is by name, with no certification binding. The token is 32 random bytes, returned once, of which only the SHA-256 digest is stored. It has **no TTL**, and revoking an enrollment does not invalidate tokens already issued — it stops new ones, which is the operation an operator actually wants. The register is append-only and hash-chained with `BEFORE UPDATE` and `BEFORE DELETE` triggers, so an enrollment or a revocation cannot be edited out of history."));
673	
674	doc.push(H3("11.2 — The gate that was added in the wrong place"));
675	doc.push(PB("The first version of the HTTP handler authenticated **after** reading and parsing the body, and the malformed-JSON branch logged the body on the way in. The combination meant an **anonymous caller could write rows to the ingest log by POSTing garbage**: send `<<<not json>>>`, the auth check is still downstream, the malformed branch fires, a row lands. Adding the credential without moving that branch would have left a write path open to anybody. The gate now runs **before the body is read at all**, and a test posts garbage with no token and asserts the ingest log still holds zero rows."));
676	doc.push(callout(
677	  "The same shape as chat-6, in a different costume",
678	  "The control existed. It was in the wrong place relative to the thing it was meant to protect. Chat-6's guard ran and its result was discarded; chat-7's check ran after the write it was guarding. Both were green, both were found by asking what happens to a hostile input rather than what happens to a valid one.",
679	  { fill: "FBEAEA", labelColor: RED },
680	));
681	
682	doc.push(H3("11.3 — The signed reviewer key directory"));
683	doc.push(PB("The attestation scheme chat-5 built has a structural property the audit stated plainly: the HMAC covers the row's `record_hash`, not the reviewer name, so the person holding a key can relabel their own attestation with somebody else's name. What stops them is that the verifier is told which reviewer each key belongs to and compares. Until chat-7 that knowledge lived on a command line — `--reviewer-key KEYID=NAME:path` — which is a name typed by a human with no signature behind it."));
684	doc.push(PB("`backend/key_directory.py` publishes the bindings as a signed document: reviewer, cert_id, key_id, registration time and revocation time for every registry row, including the revocations. It carries **no key material** — a key_id is the first sixteen hex of the key's SHA-256, which lets a recipient match a key without revealing anything that would let anyone forge an attestation. `tools/publish_key_directory.py` builds it on the Platform host and refuses if the registry chain does not verify; `tools/verify_key_directory.py` verifies it on a recipient machine importing nothing from `backend/`, the same rule the release verifier follows, and emits the `KEY_ID=REVIEWER:` fragments ready for `verify_ledger_export.py`. The recipient never types a name."));
685	doc.push(callout(
686	  "The signature is the weak guard here, and the directory knows it",
687	  "The directory is signed with the same symmetric HMAC key the bundles use, so whoever can verify it can also sign one. A signature therefore establishes that the export-key holder authored the list, not that they were entitled to. The guard that actually constrains an attacker who can sign their own directory is **consistency**: one key_id may name exactly one reviewer for its whole life. The test that matters forges a directory — one key under a second name — re-signs it so the signature passes, and the directory is still refused, by `entries_consistent`. A signature proves who wrote the file; the consistency check constrains what the file may say.",

