1:401:doc.push(P(`The build ran in nine distinct stages across ${COMMITS} commits, from 2026-11-08 00:53 to the present. The stages below follow the git history. Commit hashes and dates are taken from the log, not recollected.`));
2:555:  "Delete every row with file access, run the honest exporter, and a recipient holding the previous bundle was told every check passed — five records gone, no warning. Each check was individually correct: hashes valid, ids ordered, count matching, signature good. None compared the bundle against one the recipient already held, and an empty bundle is trivially self-consistent. My own 33 tests passed because I had not thought of the attack, so had not written it. Fixed by recording the ledger's own head in every manifest and failing when a later bundle reports a shorter ledger (no_truncation), plus genesis_anchor for rows deleted from the front and empty_bundle_justified for a zero-record export. The fix then broke the zero-record incremental export — a quiet week failed range_from — and D09 requires a weekly export, so the documented procedure would have failed on any quiet week. Both are covered by tests now.",
3:575:  "Two audits and nine material defects, with a green suite of 108 in between",
9:741:doc.push(PB("Suite 346 to 351 to **359**, re-run against HEAD at close: 359 passed, 0 failed. Per-file counts were checked individually — `test_ingest.py` 25, `test_ingest_auth.py` 18, `test_http_session.py` 9, `test_issue_reviewer_key.py` 4, `test_attribution.py` 60, `test_key_directory.py` 17 — because Stage 11 recorded what happens when the aggregate rises on tests that never ran. Both new guards are mutation-tested in isolation: each fails exactly one test when removed, and no other."));
10:963:doc.push(P("Records a decision taken 2026-11-08: AxiomDynamics retains all IP for the first five years, sells no IP rights in that window, and grants only licences. The architecture is five stages — Prove (Y1–5, licence only, BNSF first), Brand (Y5–8, exclusive white-label), Review (Y8, joint assessment), Open (Y8+, joint options with majority retained), Auction (when triggered, sole buy-out with an open bid floor)."));
11:1123:    ["tests/", "test_physics.py (physics formulas plus the A1 constants guard), test_store.py (ledger), test_export.py (signed export), test_ledger_replication.py (cadence, chaining, inspection), run_tests.py (stdlib runner, discovers test_*.py) — 114 tests", "Code"],
12:1193:doc.push(P("Stage 6 is the rebuild-and-process stage: it recovered the lost chat-1 work, installed the write-in-repo rule, and built the transcript-preservation machinery that makes this document possible. Stage 7 is chat-3, and it is the stage that changed the most after it was written — two of its four phases were materially repaired because an independent audit found what the author had not. Stage 8 is chat-4, which ran two phases: the replication series, and the model decision that closed both findings Part VII had carried since chat-3. Its most transferable lesson is in §8.3 — a numeric defect valued a missed defect 29× too high, and the test guarding it could not fail. Stage 9 is chat-5: the ledger audit, the ingest path, three supply-chain tools built but not applied, and per-disposition attribution, where an audit found a relabelling attack a green suite had been built to catch and did not. Stage 10 is chat-6, and it is the shortest demonstration of the pattern: a revocation guard written to close a race, called by a route that passed it a boolean, and tested only with a guard that raised — so the guard was inert and 310 tests were green. Stage 11 is chat-7: two AD-2 halves built, one of them gated *after* the write it was guarding, and a mutation run in which four of eighteen guards could be deleted with the suite still green. The pattern has now held across five sessions, and the varying part is only which surface the green number was lying about.", { spaceBefore: 120 }));

