import io
p='project-logs/build-history-summary.js'
s=io.open(p,encoding='utf-8').read()

anchor='// ================================================================ PART III'
block = '''// ---- Stage 13
doc.push(H2("Stage 13 — chat-9: attestation at entry, and the constraint that decided it (2026-11-13)"));
doc.push(PB("Chat-9 opened with the owner's four standing rules restated, then ran a six-rung ladder low to high. Two rungs landed: the build history caught up with chat-8, and **attestation at entry** closed the last software half of B01 §7 item 1. Suite 359 to **370**."));
doc.push(PB("The second rung is the one worth recording, because the obvious implementation cannot be built and the reason is a set of facts in the source rather than a matter of preference."));

doc.push(H3("13.1 — Three facts that rule out the obvious design"));
doc.push(PB("The shape anyone would reach for first is \\"the client sends an attestation, the server checks it.\\" It is impossible here, for three reasons that each had to be verified rather than assumed. The registry stores the **sixteen-hex `key_id`** and never the key itself. An attestation is an **HMAC** the server must compute. And it covers the row's `record_hash`, which includes `recorded_at`, `prev_hash` and `supersedes_id` — **none of which exist until the row is built inside the write transaction.** So a client cannot pre-compute an attestation for a row it has not created, and the server cannot verify one without the key bytes. Either the key is presented, or the attestation is a server-side assertion that adds no information."));
doc.push(PB("**Decision: the key is presented per request**, base64 in `X-Reviewer-Key`, and the attestation is computed and written inside the ledger's own transaction. A refused key rolls the whole write back, so there is no state in which a disposition exists that no key vouched for — not a row with a missing attestation, which would export as an honest-looking unattested record."));

doc.push(H3("13.2 — The alternative that was rejected on a property"));
doc.push(PB("Making the reviewer key the login credential would have removed the second header entirely. It was rejected because it would put the attestation secret **on the server for the life of every session**, and in every backup of the session store. A stolen session token would then be able to forge attestations, which is precisely the property the attestation exists to deny. Keeping the key separate means a stolen session alone produces no row: the caller must also hold the key file, which D09 §8.4.6 keeps off the Platform host. The reasoning is written into D09 §8.4.2a because it is the first question a security reviewer asks."));
doc.push(PB("Two layers of check are deliberate. `resolve_live_key` refuses early, so a doomed request never opens a write transaction. `stamp_attestation` re-checks under the write lock, which is the only instant at which \\"still live\\" and \\"still unwritten\\" are both true. The same shape as `resolve_session` plus the ledger guard in chat-6."));

doc.push(H3("13.3 — The demo became unusable, and that was my defect"));
doc.push(PB("`tools/seed_demo_account.py` generated the reviewer key and **discarded it** — correct before this change, because nothing required a key, and fatal after it. An operator could seed, sign in, and then have every write refused, because the only copy of the credential no longer existed. This is the same defect the chat-8 custody procedure exposed: **a procedure naming a step nobody can perform.** The fix is `--key-file`, which writes the key mode 0600 instead of printing it, and a no-flag path that says plainly that no disposition can be recorded."));

doc.push(H3("13.4 — Three assertions too loose to fail, found by mutation"));
doc.push(PB("Six mutations were written against the new guards. All six are killed now; **three survived the first run**, and every one of them was an assertion that matched the wrong message rather than a missing guard."));
doc.push(PB("M19 survived because the test asserted `\\"reviewer key\\"` appears in the error, and the *unregistered-key* message contains that too. The route's early check survived **twice**: first because asserting both reviewer names matched the inner guard's message, which also names both, and then because every route-level test was refused by the early check, so the inner layer was never reached at all. Three direct `save_disposition` tests now reach the inner guard. **Two layers of one check cannot both be tested through the outer layer** — that is the transferable rule, and it is the same family as the inert guard and the vacuous test."));
doc.push(callout(
  "The phrase that only one message contains",
  "A mutation is killed by a test that fails when the guard goes. If two guards refuse with similar wording, an assertion on a shared word kills neither. The fix each time was to assert the phrase unique to the layer under test — and the tell, three times in one session, is that the mutation passed on the first run.",
  { fill: "FFF4E0", labelColor: RED },
));

doc.push(H3("13.5 — Verified end to end, not only in unit tests"));
doc.push(PB("The full operator path was executed rather than reasoned about: `seed_demo_account --key-file` writes a 0600 key, sign-in returns a token, a write carrying that key returns 200 with `attested_by`, `verify_attestations` and `verify_chain` both report ok, and the same write without the key returns 401. The mutation driver grew from 18 to **24 mutations across five targets**, all killed, and every replacement string was checked to match its file exactly once so none is a silent no-op."));
doc.push(PB("What the stage does not claim: a credential holder is not a person, so item 1 stays open; the transport is plain HTTP (item 10); the server holds the key in memory for the duration of one request; and the export signature is still symmetric (item 11)."));

'''
assert s.count(anchor)==1
s=s.replace(anchor, block+anchor)

# counts
s=s.replace('The build ran in twelve distinct stages','The build ran in thirteen distinct stages',1)
s=s.replace('test_issue_reviewer_key.py, run_tests.py (stdlib runner, discovers test_*.py) — 359 tests',
            'test_issue_reviewer_key.py, test_session_route.py, run_tests.py (stdlib runner, discovers test_*.py) — 370 tests',1)
s=s.replace('so it now covers twelve stages and a suite of 359','so it now covers thirteen stages and a suite of 370',1)
s=s.replace('The pattern has now held across six sessions',
            'Stage 13 is chat-9: attestation at entry, where the obvious design was impossible for three reasons in the source, and three of six mutations survived on assertions too loose to distinguish one refusal from another. The pattern has now held across seven sessions',1)
io.open(p,'w',encoding='utf-8',newline='\n').write(s)
print('stage 13 added')
PYEOF
